{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786429088,"nonce":"Fji18MviGQQZzMxHJ3kzyZjmlEI=","message":"Done. Seven new files across service, api+middleware, repository (harness) and cmd/oto. Covered: correct/incorrect password, session lifetime/expiry/revocation and fail-closed resolve, PAT mint-\u003everify-\u003erevoke including a deliberate prefix collision (the constant-time compare selects the RIGHT candidate), PATs refused on session-only routes and vice versa, ingest tokens refused on the API, the login limiter tripping AND recovering, and bootstrap refusing a second run. No test sleeps — clock.Fake throughout.\n\nThe unknown-user timing defence is tested STRUCTURALLY, not by wall clock: a counting hasher asserts each of six failure paths spends EXACTLY ONE argon2id evaluation, that it was fed the submitted password rather than a constant, that no session row was written, and that all six error strings are byte-identical. A second, one-sided statistical test (real hasher, medians, \u003e=1ms floor and within-10x) is marked and skipped under -short. A tight percentage-equality timing assertion was deliberately not written — that is the flaky test that gets deleted.\n\nThe doubles are deliberately LEAKY (they filter nothing), so the service's second refusal is what the assertions observe; a filtering double would pass even if the service check were deleted. SQL predicates are pinned separately against real Postgres.\n\n⚠️ REAL DEFECT FOUND, pinned not fixed, filed separately: resolveByEmailSQL has no orgs join, while resolveSessionSQL and resolveByPrefixSQL both JOIN orgs ON deleted_at IS NULL. A member of a soft-deleted tenant still passes password verification and gets a session row written (orphans, the TokenPrefixLen class again) — and worse, the dead org's row counts toward LIMIT 2, so SOFT-DELETING A TENANT CAN LOCK OUT AN UNRELATED LIVE TENANT'S USER who shares the address, with a 401 that explains nothing. One join fixes it.\n\nAlso found: APITokenRepository.Revoke's comment promises found=false without a second round trip, but a cross-org id actually returns (false, errs.NotFound). Not exploitable — both converge on 404 — but a caller switching on found alone would be wrong.","files":null}]}